			<outline text="" created="Wed, 13 May 2026 16:42:14 GMT"/>
			<outline text="" created="Wed, 13 May 2026 16:42:16 GMT"/>
			<outline text="" created="Wed, 13 May 2026 16:42:16 GMT"/>
			<outline text="" created="Wed, 13 May 2026 16:42:17 GMT"/>
			<outline text="readme.md" created="Sun, 10 May 2026 22:39:49 GMT">
				<outline text="# RSS to JSONL">
					<outline text="Watch for new items from a set of feeds."/>
					<outline text="Add each item to the end of a JSONL file on a static server."/>
					<outline text="A live example is the JSONL feed for scripting.com: [scripting.jsonl](https://jsonldemo.feedland.org/scripting.jsonl)."/>
					<outline text="If you have a JSONL client, you can test. Does this make you think of applications? Any way we can help, we have lots of feed resources here. Use the Issues section here to share."/>
					<outline text="Here's a [blog post](http://scripting.com/2026/05/12.html#a130150) about it."/>
					</outline>
				<outline text="### Why JSONL">
					<outline text="JSONL is the standard &quot;stream of records&quot; format across the AI and ML toolchain. One JSON object per line, append-only, trivially streamable. The AI side already speaks it natively."/>
					<outline text="A few places JSONL is the default input:">
						<outline text="OpenAI and Anthropic fine-tuning APIs."/>
						<outline text="OpenAI Batch API and Anthropic Message Batches API."/>
						<outline text="HuggingFace datasets."/>
						<outline text="Pandas, DuckDB, Polars, Spark."/>
						<outline text="LangChain and LlamaIndex document loaders."/>
						</outline>
					<outline text="So an RSS-to-JSONL feed plugs into any of these with no adapter code. RSS items flow into the AI world as a native format. That's the interop story."/>
					</outline>
				<outline text="### Install and run">
					<outline text="`npm install` to pull dependencies."/>
					<outline text="`node rsstojsonl.js` to start."/>
					</outline>
				<outline text="### Configuration">
					<outline text="Defaults live in `rsstojsonl.js`. Create a `config.json` next to it to override any of the keys. Only the keys you list are overwritten."/>
					<outline text="Main keys:">
						<outline text="`urlFeedlandSocket`: the FeedLand server to subscribe to."/>
						<outline text="`dataFolder`: local folder where the JSONL files are appended."/>
						<outline text="`maxLinesPerJsonlFile`: most recent N lines kept in each file."/>
						<outline text="`sets`: one entry per output file. Each set has `enabled`, `jsonlFilename`, `jsonlPath`, `jsonlUrl`, and a `feedList` of source feed URLs."/>
						</outline>
					</outline>
				<outline text="### S3">
					<outline text="The app uploads each updated JSONL file to S3 via `daves3`. You'll need AWS credentials configured the way `daves3` expects them. Without that, the upload step fails on every new item."/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		<outline text="build script" created="Wed, 13 May 2026 16:00:13 GMT">
			<outline text="nodeEditorSuite.utilities.buildRsstojsonl ()" created="Wed, 10 Jan 2024 21:45:16 GMT"/>
			</outline>
		</body>
	</opml>
